  "ideographicDescription": "⿰耳又[GV]",
  "confidence": 93,
  "verificationStatus": "cross-verified",
  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A hand 又 taking the ear 耳 of a fallen enemy"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a hand (又) grabbing an ear (耳). In ancient China, the ears of opponents in battle were cut off and collected as tokens of victory."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "捕取也。从又从耳。《周禮》：“獲者取左耳。”《司馬法》曰：“載獻聝。”聝者，耳也。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 耳 (“ear”) + 又 (“hand”) - to take the ear of fallen prey, a rite of hunters mentioned in the Rites of Zhou.\nShuowen interprets it as taking the ear of an enemy instead. However, Wang (2022) points out that there is no evidence that this actually happened in war."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "捕取也。从又从耳。《周禮》：“獲者取左耳。”《司馬法》曰：“載獻聝。”聝者，耳也。",
  "shuowenExplanation": "To capture and to take. Composed of 又 'hand' and 耳 'ear'. The 周禮》：“獲者取左耳。”《司馬法 says: \"載獻聝。\". Take; Ear.",
